<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.vnx.subsystem-probe</string>
  <!--
    absence-is-loud, punt 1: governance-enforcement-stack and plan-gate-panel
    read stale in t0_state from 12 July to 24 September 2026. Their producer,
    subsystem_health.aggregate(), writes one health beacon per subsystem and
    only ran when someone typed `vnx subsystems` with the probe flag. Both
    probes work and measure real values. Nothing drove them. This job is the
    driver, nothing else: it runs the probes on a cadence. It does not change
    a probe, does not gate anything and does not touch the checkout.

    Interval: every 6h (21600s), the same reasoning as com.vnx.ledger-health.
    aggregate() builds its beacons with HealthBeacon's default
    expected_interval_seconds of 86400 (it passes none), and
    health_beacon.all_beacons() reads a beacon older than that as "stale". A
    StartInterval at or above 86400 would make the beacons stale by
    construction. 21600s gives 4 runs a day: 3 consecutive missed runs (an 18h
    gap) still land inside the window. RunAtLoad makes the first run happen on
    install, so the beacons are fresh right away and not after one interval.

    Command: the same entry point `vnx subsystems` runs. scripts/commands/
    subsystems.sh is a thin wrapper around `python3 -m vnx_cli.main
    subsystems`, and this job names that module directly instead of going
    through bin/vnx, for two reasons. bin/vnx unsets VNX_HOME and PROJECT_ROOT
    and re-derives them from its own location and the working directory, so
    the environment below would stop being the source of truth. And behind
    bin/vnx the interpreter is invisible to the PATH-parity guard
    (scripts/lib/path_parity.py reads "no python invocation found"); naming
    python3 here lets it check the interpreter against requires-python.

    Where the beacons land: the module resolves the project from
    VNX_PROJECT_ID and the data directory from that id (the central
    per-project store when it exists), and writes each beacon to
    <data_dir>/health/<subsystem>.json.
    That is the directory all_beacons() reads. Nothing lands in state/health.
    Measured with a tmp HOME and a tmp VNX_DATA_DIR in
    tests/test_launchd_subsystem_probe_driver.py, which runs this command as
    installed.

    VNX_HOME must be the primary checkout, never a dispatch worktree. The
    probes measure the checkout they run from: the governance probe reads
    .vnx-attest/plan-gates.ndjson at that checkout's git root and the plan-gate
    probe reads the coordination database in that checkout's own data
    directory.
    .vnx-attest is git-ignored, so a worktree or a fresh clone has none. The
    probes then report "unknown", unknown writes no beacon and the job still
    exits 0, so the beacons would go stale again without a sound. This is also
    why the Label is bare: the job measures a checkout, not a project, so
    there is one instance per machine. A per-project Label would make every
    project's t0_state expect an instance that has nothing to measure for it.

    Exit status is not proof. The subsystems command catches an error inside
    the probe run, renders every row as a bare "unknown" and still exits 0. A
    run that failed shows up as beacons going stale after 24h, and in
    /tmp/vnx-subsystem-probe.log as governance-enforcement-stack and
    plan-gate-panel printing a bare "unknown" where a working run prints a
    status and a signal.

    vnx init is not wired to install this job. Install it manually, from the
    primary checkout:
      bash scripts/launchd/reload_plist.sh com.vnx.subsystem-probe
    reload_plist.sh substitutes the placeholders below from VNX_HOME and from
    its optional second argument, then VNX_PROJECT_ID, then the
    .vnx-project-id marker.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>cd ${VNX_HOME} &amp;&amp; exec python3 -m vnx_cli.main subsystems --probe</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>21600</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-subsystem-probe.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-subsystem-probe.err</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>VNX_HOME</key>
    <string>${VNX_HOME}</string>
    <key>VNX_PROJECT_ID</key>
    <string>${VNX_PROJECT_ID}</string>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
